From c5ec5f366bde35724471b043212897c6387b83ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 15:20:14 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: publish credentials, file identities and file
 hashes

A call's arguments say what a program asked for; three kinds of
lifecycle record say what it got. Each is one fixed, versioned
structure published with the call it belongs to.

Credentials (lifecycle.creds, antinomie.creds/2, 96 bytes): every
traced task's uids and gids, audit login uid and session, all five
capability sets and the securebits, at its first traced call and after
every change, with the reason (setuid family, capset, prctl, an exec of
a set-id or file-capability image, loginuid).

File identity (lifecycle.file_id, antinomie.fileid/1, 32 bytes): the
device, inode, generation, mode and link count of the file an open
returned, a rename moved, a link named, or an unlink or rmdir removed,
so the host can follow one file across its names and tell a reused
name from the same file. lifecycle.exec_image names the image an exec
loaded and, for a script, the interpreter as its #! line names it.
io_uring and AIO requests carry the same identity as their operand: an
OPENAT the file it opened, a read, write, sync, splice or xattr request
the regular file it used, and a RENAMEAT, LINKAT or UNLINKAT the
identity its operation published, with the links it left.

Content hashes (lifecycle.file_hash, antinomie.hash/1 and /2): a worker
computes the SHA-256 of the image an exec committed and of a file about
to be unlinked, through a private read-only open with no notification
and no access-time update, from a bounded queue (16 jobs, 64 MiB, files
up to 16 MiB), and publishes the digest or why it was skipped under the
identity of the call that caused it. The file's state at the call is
compared with its state after the read, and CHANGED reports any
difference; digests are cached only on filesystems with a change
counter. With the new EXEC flags, HASH_WRITES hashes each regular file
the run wrote when its last writer lets it go, and HASH_ROOT hashes the
launched program's own image. TRACER_CAPS tells a host which optional
EXEC flags this guest accepts and its hashing budget.

Hooked kernel files:
- kernel/cred.c (commit_creds()) and kernel/audit.c
  (audit_set_loginuid()): every credential change of a task goes
  through one of them.
- fs/open.c, fs/namei.c (do_unlinkat(), do_rmdir(), do_linkat(),
  do_renameat2()): where the call has the inode in hand, after it
  succeeded; do_unlinkat() also records the file's state for its hash
  before vfs_unlink().
- fs/exec.c: the exec commit, for the image's identity and hash.
- fs/file_table.c (__fput()): the last reference of a file opened for
  writing, which is when its last writer has let it go.
- fs/aio.c, io_uring/io_uring.c, io_uring/io_uring.h,
  io_uring/openclose.c, io_uring/splice.c and io_uring/fs.c: the
  requests' files; io_uring/fs.c marks the request a worker runs
  (task_struct ant_async_target, cleared in kernel/fork.c) so the
  identity its rename, link or unlink publishes goes to that request.
- kernel/antinomie/Kconfig selects CRYPTO_LIB_SHA256.

Contract: revision 17 (credentials and hashes). File identity, the exec
image, HASH_WRITES, HASH_ROOT and TRACER_CAPS are additive within it:
an earlier guest answers TRACER_CAPS with -EOPNOTSUPP and must not be
sent the flags.
